{"reviewer_participant_id":"actor:dependence-free-batch-f1-reviewer-fable-28","reviews":[{"case_id":"case:ca3125c6ca6002055d70","verdict":"demonstrated_issue","issue_class":"issue-class:repeated-authorized-independent-unit-entry-into-row-independent-procedure","bounded_statement":"Within the supplied files for case:ca3125c6ca6002055d70, the selected result — an exact two-sided binomial test declaring a statistically significant bias away from the UV-marked arm (5 of 20 bouts, p = 0.041389) — is not supported, because the 20 rows entered as independent Bernoulli trials are repeated bouts from only 6 independent units (bee_id), each contributing 2–4 rows. The binomial test assumes row independence; the report's own per-bee tallies show strong within-bee consistency (three bees chose 'plain' in all 11 of their combined bouts, one bee chose 'uv_marked' in both of its bouts), and at the bee level only 1 of 6 bees showed a majority UV-marked preference, so the significance claim depends on the pseudoreplicated n = 20 rather than the 6 independent units.","root_cause":"The workflow counts every row of data/input.csv as one Bernoulli trial (n_bouts = len(bouts); n_marked summed over rows) and passes these row-level counts directly to scipy.stats.binomtest(n_marked, n_bouts, 0.5), a procedure whose sampling model requires each of the n trials to be an independent draw. Because the data description designates bee_id as the independent-unit column and each of the 6 bees is authorized to appear in 2–4 rows by the repeated-release design, the same independent unit repeatedly enters the row-independent procedure, inflating the effective sample size from 6 units to 20 rows and producing an unwarranted p < 0.05.","evidence_atoms":[{"description":"The data description names bee_id as the independent-unit column and states that a row is a single bout, with the same bees re-released so each bee appears in multiple rows (2–4 bouts per bee).","observed_value":"Independent unit column: bee_id; 6 bees with 2–4 rows each","source_spans":[{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":21,"end_line":23,"quoted_text":"One row is: one foraging bout flown by one marked bumblebee at the two-armed array\nIndependent unit column: bee_id\nOne trial is: one row"},{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":7,"end_line":9,"quoted_text":"The same bees were\nre-released several times across three mornings, so an individual bee appears in\nmore than one row of the table (between two and four bouts per bee)."}]},{"description":"The workflow tallies successes and trials over rows (bouts), not over bees, and submits the row-level counts to an exact binomial test, a procedure that assumes each trial is independent.","observed_value":"binomtest(n_marked=5, n_bouts=20, 0.5) with n counted over rows","source_spans":[{"path":"workflow/analysis.py","start_line":39,"end_line":40,"quoted_text":"    n_bouts = len(bouts)\n    n_marked = sum(1 for bout in bouts if bout[\"first_arm\"] == MARKED_ARM)"},{"path":"workflow/analysis.py","start_line":45,"end_line":45,"quoted_text":"    outcome = binomtest(n_marked, n_bouts, 0.5, alternative=\"two-sided\")"}]},{"description":"The report's selected result claims statistical significance at the 5% level based on the 20 pseudoreplicated row-level trials.","observed_value":"p = 0.041389 on n = 20 rows from 6 bees","source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":30,"end_line":30,"quoted_text":"[selected-result] Exact two-sided binomial test on 20 scored bouts: 5 of 20 first choices went to the UV-marked arm (25.0%), p = 0.041389 against the null proportion of 0.5, indicating a statistically significant bias away from the UV-marked arm at the 5% level."},{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":24,"end_line":26,"quoted_text":"Every scored bout was entered as one Bernoulli trial, and the 20 trials were submitted\nto an exact two-sided binomial test (`scipy.stats.binomtest`) against the\nno-preference expectation of 0.5."}]},{"description":"The report's own per-bee table demonstrates that rows are not independent within bees: three bees chose 'plain' on every one of their combined 11 bouts and one bee chose 'uv_marked' on both of its bouts, so repeated rows from a bee are highly correlated. At the bee level only 1 of 6 bees (B04) has a majority UV-marked share, which cannot yield p < 0.05 in any exact 6-unit test.","observed_value":"per-bee uv_marked shares: 0.000, 0.000, 0.000, 1.000, 0.500, 0.333","source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":15,"end_line":20,"quoted_text":"| B01 | 4 | 0 | 0.000 |\n| B02 | 3 | 0 | 0.000 |\n| B03 | 4 | 0 | 0.000 |\n| B04 | 2 | 2 | 1.000 |\n| B05 | 4 | 2 | 0.500 |\n| B06 | 3 | 1 | 0.333 |"}]},{"description":"The raw table confirms the repeated entry of the same independent unit: bee B01 alone contributes four rows, all with first_arm = plain, and the 20 data rows span only bee IDs B01–B06.","observed_value":"B01 appears in 4 consecutive rows, all 'plain'","source_spans":[{"path":"data/input.csv","start_line":2,"end_line":5,"quoted_text":"B01,1,2025-06-03,A2,plain,7.4\nB01,2,2025-06-03,A2,plain,6.9\nB01,3,2025-06-04,A2,plain,8.1\nB01,4,2025-06-04,A2,plain,7.7"}]}],"counterevidence_atoms":[{"description":"The data description states 'One trial is: one row', which could be read as authorizing a row-level trial definition; however, this line describes what a row records, while the same description explicitly designates bee_id (not the row) as the independent-unit column, so it does not license treating rows as independent draws in a binomial test.","observed_value":"One trial is: one row","source_spans":[{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":22,"end_line":23,"quoted_text":"Independent unit column: bee_id\nOne trial is: one row"}]},{"description":"The report transparently discloses the repeated-measures structure — it states there are only 6 individually marked bees for the 20 bouts and prints a full per-bee tally — so the pseudoreplication is not concealed; nevertheless, the inferential test and the significance claim are computed on the 20 rows, not the 6 units.","observed_value":"20 bouts, 6 bees disclosed alongside an n=20 test","source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":6,"end_line":7,"quoted_text":"- Foraging bouts scored: 20\n- Individually marked bees: 6"},{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":11,"end_line":14,"quoted_text":"### Bouts per bee\n\n| bee_id | bouts | uv_marked first | share |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |"}]}],"falsification_attempt":"I tried to defeat the finding in three ways. (1) Computation check: I recomputed the exact two-sided binomial p-value for 5 successes in 20 trials against 0.5 — P(X<=5)+P(X>=15) = 2*21700/1048576 = 0.041389 — which matches the report, ruling out the alternative explanation that the significance claim is a mere arithmetic error rather than a unit-of-analysis error. (2) Independence check: I asked whether the 20 rows could plausibly behave as independent trials despite sharing bee IDs; the per-bee tallies refute this, since bees B01–B03 chose 'plain' in all 11 of their bouts and B04 chose 'uv_marked' in both of its bouts, a pattern showing strong within-bee correlation of outcomes. (3) Robustness check: I asked whether the conclusion would survive analysis at the designated independent unit; with 6 bees, only 1 has a majority UV-marked share (B04), 3 have zero, and a sign test or exact binomial on 6 units with any reasonable dichotomization cannot reach p < 0.05 (minimum attainable two-sided p with n=6 is 2/64 ≈ 0.031, requiring a 6-0 or 0-6 split, which does not occur). All three attempts failed to overturn the finding, so the significant conclusion is attributable to repeated entry of the 6 authorized independent units (bee_id) into the row-independent binomial procedure.","cross_case_evidence_used":false,"unresolved_material_questions":[],"self_reported_confidence":"high"}]}